<% content_for :title, "Veglia" %>

<div class="w-full max-w-5xl mx-auto pb-16">
  <header class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-8">
    <div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Veglia</h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">Keeping watch on the connection</p>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 tabular-nums" data-refresh-at="<%= @generated_at.to_i %>">
      as of <%= local_time(@generated_at) %>
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="space-y-8">
    <%= render partial: "node", collection: @summaries, as: :summary %>
  </div>

  <footer class="mt-10 text-xs text-slate-400 dark:text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
    <p>
      Uptime is measured against the minutes Veglia actually observed. Coverage is
      how many of the window's minutes those were: a monitor cannot record its own
      downtime, so the two numbers are reported side by side and never combined.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Minutes marked <span class="font-medium text-violet-600 dark:text-violet-400">not observed</span>
      are counted as a failure in their own right, and a separate one from the connection
      dropping. Veglia was supposed to be watching and was not, which is worth fixing
      whatever the link was doing at the time, and is not something the colour of a
      good minute should be allowed to cover.
    </p>
  </footer>
</div>

<script>
  // Reload on a slow cadence, and only while the tab is actually being
  // looked at, so a dashboard left open on a spare screen does not
  // spend the night waking the machine it is meant to be watching.
  setInterval(() => {
    if (document.visibilityState === "visible") window.location.reload();
  }, 30000);
</script>
